<section class="hero" id="about">
 <div class="intro"><h1 class="hero-title">Baojuan<br/>Wang</h1><p class="name-cn">王宝娟</p><p class="position">Ph.D. student in Computer Science<br/>Drexel University</p><div class="quick-links"><a href="mailto:bw689@drexel.edu">Email</a><a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=GTa0UdgAAAAJ&amp;hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Scholar</a><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/baojuan-wang/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a></div></div>
 <div class="bio"><p>I am a Ph.D. student in Computer Science at <a href="https://drexel.edu/">Drexel University</a>, where I am a member of <a href="https://www.sepalab.com/">SePAL</a> and am advised by <a href="https://zhibosun.com/">Dr. Zhibo Eric Sun</a> and co-advised by <a href="https://yue.zyueinfosec.com/">Dr. Yue Zhang</a>. Before joining Drexel, I earned my M.S. in Computer Science from <a href="https://www.cqut.edu.cn/">Chongqing University of Technology</a>, advised by <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=qysA0NoAAAAJ&amp;hl=en">Dr. Wanping Liu</a>. I earned B.S. degrees in Statistics and Computer Science from <a href="https://www.ytu.edu.cn/">Yantai University</a>.</p><p>My research focuses on human-centered security and privacy, particularly on how people perceive, interpret, and act on security information. I study how interactions with security systems and AI assistance shape users’ judgment, trust, learning, and behavior, and how LLMs are changing security practice by influencing decision-making, introducing new risks, and creating new opportunities for security and privacy support.</p></div>
</section>
<section class="research" id="research"><h2 class="section-title">Research</h2>
 <article class="work"><div class="work-content"><p class="publication-year">ACM CCS 2026 (to appear)</p><h3 class="work-title">Learning Less While Asking More: How LLM-Based Assistance Shapes Vulnerability Learning</h3><p class="author-line"><strong>Baojuan Wang</strong>, Yifan Yao, Yue Zhang, Zhibo Eric Sun</p><p class="work-desc">We compared LLM chat and web search in a study of 70 people learning about software vulnerabilities. While participants rated LLM assistance more favorably, it was associated with smaller gains in understanding vulnerability causes, runtime behavior, and security consequences. We also designed and piloted VulnScaffold, a structured learning interface.</p><div class="linkline"><a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22287391" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Artifact</a></div></div><figure class="work-art"><img id="ccs-art" alt="Illustrative code and LLM chat prompts emphasizing frequent fix-oriented questions, with less emphasis on understanding causes" /><figcaption>LLM learners often shifted toward fix-oriented questions rather than sustained explanation.</figcaption></figure></article>
 <article class="work work--reverse"><div class="work-content"><p class="publication-year">IEEE/ACM IWQoS 2026</p><h3 class="work-title">The Imitation Game: Using Large Language Models to Disrupt Chinese Chat-Based Cybercrime</h3><p class="author-line">Yifan Yao, <strong>Baojuan Wang</strong>, Jinhao Duan, Kaidi Xu, Chuankai Guo, Zhibo Eric Sun, Yue Zhang</p><p class="work-desc">We developed LURE to investigate chat-based cybercrime through supervised, LLM-driven conversations. In a study spanning 98 Telegram groups, the system engaged 53 suspected service providers. These interactions helped us examine their communication and payment practices.</p><div class="linkline"><a class="iwqos-pdf" href="assets/pdf/iwqos2026.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" hidden>Paper (PDF)</a></div></div><figure class="work-art"><img id="lure-art" alt="Concept illustration of LURE: a supervised conversational agent and a workflow of discovery, engagement, and analysis" /><figcaption>LURE combines account discovery, supervised conversations, and payment-related analysis.</figcaption></figure></article>
</section>
<section class="publications" id="publications"><h2 class="section-title">Publications</h2><ol class="paper-list"><li><span class="year">2026</span><div><p class="ptitle">Learning Less While Asking More: How LLM-Based Assistance Shapes Vulnerability Learning</p><p class="pdetail"><strong>Baojuan Wang</strong>, Yifan Yao, Yue Zhang, Zhibo Eric Sun. <em>ACM CCS 2026 (to appear).</em> </p></div></li><li><span class="year">2026</span><div><p class="ptitle">The Imitation Game: Using Large Language Models to Disrupt Chinese Chat-Based Cybercrime</p><p class="pdetail">Yifan Yao, <strong>Baojuan Wang</strong>, Jinhao Duan, Kaidi Xu, Chuankai Guo, Zhibo Eric Sun, Yue Zhang. <em>IEEE/ACM IWQoS 2026.</em> <a class="iwqos-pdf" href="assets/pdf/iwqos2026.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" hidden>Paper (PDF)</a></p></div></li><li><span class="year">2024</span><div><p class="ptitle">CFOA: Exploring Transferable Adversarial Examples by Content Feature Optimization with Attention Guidance</p><p class="pdetail">Wanping Liu, <strong>Baojuan Wang</strong>, Dong Huang, Haolan Luo, Ling Lu. <em>Computers &amp; Security, 142, 103882.</em> <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167404824001834" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paper</a></p></div></li></ol></section>

